in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @epistemologic
in @glue

# @epistemologic/math/cholesky — the Cholesky decomposition theorem
# as a predicate on matrix structure at @epistemologic/math altitude.
#
# === The recognition ===
#
# Per `docs/specs/reality-algebra-math-and-glue.md` §P0 (Alex directive
# 2026-07-01): the Cholesky theorem is a PREDICATE on matrix
# structure — "for symmetric positive-definite A, A = LL^T" — not a
# matrix object. Predicates about mathematical objects live at
# `@epistemologic/math/*` (per [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-
# word]]: the substrate has been carrying this partition since
# `@epistemologic/math/curvature` `@epistemologic/math/music`
# `@epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian` landed).
#
# The Cholesky arc composes four shards end-to-end:
#
#   1. THIS SHARD — @epistemologic/math/cholesky
#      The theorem shard (predicate: symmetric-positive-definite ⇒
#      LL^T decomposition).
#   2. `shards/glue/math_silicon.mirror` — the @glue species per
#      `reality-algebra-math-and-glue.md` §3.3.1 (the Mesland
#      correspondence carrying `morphism_kind: @arxiv/math/cholesky_
#      decomposition.lapack_realization`).
#   3. `shards/reality/algebra/silicon.mirror` (Mara `9ca6723`, LANDED)
#      — the silicon species; the target altitude the theorem
#      discharges into via @glue/math_silicon.
#   4. LAPACK dpotrf / dpotrs FFI binding — Reed's discharge boundary.
#
# This shard is INPUT to the composition; the @glue species is the
# composition MECHANISM; the silicon species is the TARGET altitude;
# the LAPACK binding is where @io receives the realization.
#
# === Cholesky 1910 ===
#
# André-Louis Cholesky (1875-1918), French military geodesist. Devised
# the algorithm ~1910 for solving geodetic normal equations under least-
# squares adjustment; the manuscript was published POSTHUMOUSLY by
# Benoît in 1924 (`Sur la méthode de résolution des équations normales
# provenant de l'application de la méthode des moindres carrés à un
# système d'équations linéaires en nombre inférieur à celui des
# inconnues (procédé du Commandant Cholesky)`, Bull. Géodésique 2:67-
# 77). The substrate cites the underlying discipline; the theorem is:
#
#   For any symmetric positive-definite matrix A ∈ R^{n×n} there exists
#   a UNIQUE lower-triangular matrix L ∈ R^{n×n} with strictly positive
#   diagonal entries such that A = L · L^T.
#
# The theorem's constructive proof IS the Cholesky algorithm (the
# recursive elimination Cholesky named). The uniqueness is what makes
# LL^T a content-addressable normal form on the SPD cone. The strict-
# positivity of the diagonal is what makes the decomposition NUMERICALLY
# stable — LAPACK's `dpotrf` returns the decomposition; on failure
# (non-positive-definite A) it reports the leading minor at which
# positive-definiteness broke.
#
# The complex extension: for Hermitian positive-definite A ∈ C^{n×n}
# there exists a unique lower-triangular L ∈ C^{n×n} with real strictly
# positive diagonal such that A = L · L^*. LAPACK's `zpotrf` discharges
# this variant. This shard's predicates admit both readings via the
# `field` parameter on the matrix type carriers.
#
# === Why this lives at @epistemologic/math, not at @reality/algebra/math ===
#
# Per `reality-algebra-math-and-glue.md` §P0 the object-vs-predicate
# partition is another instance of #55/#61 (form/process partition at
# family-root altitude) recurring at sub-shard altitude:
#
#   `@reality/algebra/math` — mathematical OBJECTS at family-root
#     altitude. `math_crystal` is form-side (a typed record for one
#     object); a specific matrix instance lives here.
#
#   `@epistemologic/math/*` — PREDICATES / THEOREMS about mathematical
#     objects at property altitude. Cholesky is a predicate on the
#     structure of symmetric-positive-definite matrices; it lives
#     alongside `@epistemologic/math/curvature` (per-edge Balanced
#     Forman reading), `@epistemologic/math/music` (harmonic-decidable
#     family), `@epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian` (Δ_F = δ*δ
#     construction + λ₀ predicate).
#
# The theorem shard NAMES the predicate; the @glue species carries the
# correspondence between the theorem and its silicon realization; the
# silicon species targets the LAPACK routine.
#
# === Bridge to @glue/math_silicon ===
#
# The `discharge_via` action below is the substrate-decl edge that
# points at the @glue/math_silicon Mesland correspondence. Per
# `reality-algebra-math-and-glue.md` §2.1 the correspondence's
# `morphism_kind` ref is `@arxiv/math/cholesky_decomposition.lapack_
# realization`; this shard forward-references that kind via
# `discharge_via` so consumers can locate the composition point
# without re-declaring the correspondence at this altitude.
#
# @glue.translate(c_math_silicon, cholesky_instance) is the composed
# operation that:
#   1. Consults @fate to select the LAPACK realization (dpotrf vs
#      zpotrf; LDL^T vs LL^T; in-place vs out-of-place) within the
#      correspondence's restriction (precision: f64/f32/complex;
#      conditioning: well-conditioned; layout: column-major).
#   2. Applies the selected realization via FFI to the SPD input.
#   3. Returns the imperfect<result, ref, transparency(precision)>
#      wrapping the (L, verdict, opacity) triple.
#
# The predicate shard does NOT invoke @glue; it names the predicate
# and its discharge_via edge; consumers pull the composition when
# they need the empirical realization.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   Cholesky decomposition   Cholesky ~1910; Benoît 1924 (Bull.
#                            Géodésique 2:67-77, posthumous
#                            publication). The theorem statement +
#                            constructive algorithm this shard
#                            names.
#   Symmetric matrices       Sylvester 1852 (`A demonstration of the
#                            theorem that every homogeneous quadratic
#                            polynomial is reducible by real
#                            orthogonal substitutions to the form
#                            of a sum of positive and negative
#                            squares`); Sylvester's law of inertia
#                            grounds the positive-definiteness
#                            reading.
#   Positive-definite forms  Cauchy 1829 (via the quadratic-form
#                            spectrum work grounding
#                            `@epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian`
#                            's eigenvalue predicate); Gram 1881
#                            (`Über die Entwicklung reeller
#                            Functionen in Reihen mittelst der
#                            Methode der kleinsten Quadrate`,
#                            J. reine angew. Math.  94:41-73) —
#                            the Gram-matrix ancestor of the SPD
#                            cone.
#   Triangular factorization Turing 1948 (`Rounding-off errors in
#                            matrix processes`, Quart. J. Mech. Appl.
#                            Math. 1:287-308) — the numerical-
#                            stability discipline the LAPACK
#                            realization inherits; Wilkinson 1961
#                            (`Error analysis of direct methods of
#                            matrix inversion`, J. ACM 8:281-330) —
#                            the modern floating-point analysis.
#   LAPACK dpotrf / dpotrs   Anderson et al. 1992 (`LAPACK Users'
#                            Guide`, SIAM); the reference numerical-
#                            algebra library the substrate discharges
#                            to via @glue/math_silicon.
#
# The substrate's contribution is *typing* the theorem as a predicate
# shard at @epistemologic/math altitude so the LAPACK realization's
# substrate-altitude home is the @glue/math_silicon correspondence;
# no invention.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/reality-algebra-math-and-glue.md §P0
#               (Alex directive 2026-07-01 correcting placement to
#               @epistemologic/math); §2.1 (the math ↔ silicon
#               Mesland correspondence; morphism_kind ref);
#               §3.3.1 (the @glue/math_silicon shard shape);
#               §5.3 (the first-empirical-crystal path);
#               docs/specs/cascade-ffi-runtime-link.md §7 (Q4 LAPACK
#               FFI case; Reed's dpotrf binding work operationalizes
#               here).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-connes-spectral-triple]],
#                      [[architecture-operations-as-linear-algebra]],
#                      [[architecture-form-process-partition-at-
#                       family-root]] (#55; the object-vs-predicate
#                       partition #P0 named is a sub-shard instance
#                       of the family-root partition — @reality/
#                       algebra/math carries objects, @epistemologic
#                       /math carries predicates about them),
#                      [[architecture-flang-mirror-numerical-split]]
#                       (the D→flang dispatch discipline; LAPACK is
#                       the flang-altitude realization),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (spd_matrix and
#                       lower_triangular_matrix are typed carriers,
#                       not bare matrix aliases),
#                      [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]
#                       (the @epistemologic/math predicate altitude
#                       was already carrying curvature, music,
#                       sheaf_laplacian; Cholesky joins the family).
# Related shards: shards/glue/math_silicon.mirror (the @glue species
#                  the discharge_via edge points at; the composition
#                  mechanism that translates this predicate into
#                  LAPACK invocations),
#                 shards/reality/algebra/math.mirror (the OBJECT-side
#                  sibling family-root; math_crystal carries specific
#                  matrix instances; this shard's predicates ABOUT
#                  matrices are the process-side counterpart),
#                 shards/reality/algebra/silicon.mirror (Mara
#                  `9ca6723`; the target altitude the discharge lands
#                  at via @glue/math_silicon),
#                 shards/epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian.mirror
#                  (sibling predicate at same altitude; grounds the
#                  eigenvalue reading Cholesky implicitly relies on
#                  through positive-definiteness),
#                 shards/epistemologic/math/curvature.mirror (sibling
#                  predicate; the per-edge Balanced Forman reading),
#                 shards/epistemologic/math/music.mirror (sibling
#                  predicate at same altitude, family-root only),
#                 glass.mirror (the splinter floor; imperfect carrier
#                  wraps the discharge_via result).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/epistemologic/math/cholesky.mirror` declares
# `@epistemologic/math/cholesky` per the
# `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace` rule.

source @arxiv/math/cholesky-1910
source @arxiv/math/benoit-1924
source @arxiv/math/sylvester-1852
source @arxiv/math/gram-1881
source @arxiv/math/turing-1948
source @arxiv/math/wilkinson-1961
source @arxiv/math/anderson-lapack-1992

prism @epistemologic/math/cholesky <= @epistemologic/math {
  focus cholesky
  project cholesky
  split cholesky
  shift cholesky
  settle cholesky
}

# === The spd_matrix predicate carrier ===
#
# A typed record per [[feedback-no-bare-types]] naming the
# symmetric-positive-definite matrix precondition Cholesky's theorem
# quantifies over. Not a matrix instance — a predicate carrier
# naming the STRUCTURAL PROPERTY (symmetry + positive-definiteness)
# a matrix instance must satisfy for the theorem to apply.
#
# - `field`          the typed ref naming the scalar field
#                    (`@arxiv/math/real` for R^{n×n} case discharging
#                    to LAPACK `dpotrf`; `@arxiv/math/complex` for
#                    C^{n×n} Hermitian case discharging to `zpotrf`).
# - `dimension`      the u32 cardinal of the matrix rank (n for the
#                    n × n matrix).
# - `symmetric`      the verdict that A = A^T (or A = A^* in the
#                    complex Hermitian case).
# - `positive_def`   the verdict that x^T A x > 0 for every nonzero
#                    x ∈ field^n. The strict inequality is what
#                    makes LL^T's diagonal strictly positive and the
#                    decomposition NUMERICALLY stable.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (field, dimension, symmetric,
# positive_def). The realisation layer discharges the verdicts
# against a specific matrix instance; the substrate names the
# predicate.
type spd_matrix = {
  field:        ref,
  dimension:    u32,
  symmetric:    verdict,
  positive_def: verdict,
}

# === The lower_triangular_matrix predicate carrier ===
#
# A typed record per [[feedback-no-bare-types]] naming the
# lower-triangular structural property Cholesky's theorem quantifies
# over on the OUTPUT side. Not a matrix instance — a predicate
# carrier naming the STRUCTURAL PROPERTY (lower-triangular form +
# strictly-positive diagonal) the theorem's L must satisfy.
#
# - `field`          the typed ref naming the scalar field; must
#                    match the source spd_matrix's field for the
#                    theorem to compose.
# - `dimension`      the u32 cardinal; must match the source
#                    spd_matrix's dimension.
# - `lower_tri`      the verdict that L_ij = 0 for j > i.
# - `pos_diagonal`   the verdict that L_ii > 0 for every i.
#                    Strictly positive; not merely non-negative.
#                    This is what makes the decomposition UNIQUE
#                    (the L cone is not modulo sign).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (field, dimension, lower_tri,
# pos_diagonal).
type lower_triangular_matrix = {
  field:        ref,
  dimension:    u32,
  lower_tri:    verdict,
  pos_diagonal: verdict,
}

# === The cholesky_decomposition witness carrier ===
#
# A typed record per [[feedback-no-bare-types]] carrying the
# (input, output, equality-witness) triple that instantiates the
# theorem for a specific matrix pair. Substrate-altitude form of
# "A = L · L^T"; the realisation layer discharges the equality
# against IEEE-754 floating-point semantics.
#
# - `input`      the spd_matrix predicate carrier the theorem's
#                hypothesis quantifies over.
# - `output`     the lower_triangular_matrix predicate carrier the
#                theorem's conclusion quantifies over.
# - `equality`   the verdict witnessing A = L · L^T (or A = L · L^*
#                in the Hermitian case). The realisation layer
#                discharges via LAPACK's own residual check or via
#                the substrate's numerical predicate library.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (input, output, equality).
type cholesky_decomposition = {
  input:    spd_matrix,
  output:   lower_triangular_matrix,
  equality: verdict,
}

# === verify — the theorem's constructive verifier ===
#
# Given an spd_matrix predicate + a candidate lower_triangular_matrix
# predicate, verifies whether the pair witnesses Cholesky's theorem
# — i.e., whether A = L · L^T holds within the numerical tolerance
# of the field. Returns the verdict.
#
# Pure; deterministic per IEEE-754 semantics on the platform. Body
# discharges at the realisation boundary (LAPACK's residual check
# or the substrate's numerical predicate library).
#
# This action is what @fate consultations at the math altitude call
# when browsing prior cholesky_decomposition crystals in the
# Bauchladen; the verdict is the cache-hit criterion.
verify(a: spd_matrix, l: lower_triangular_matrix) -> verdict { \ }

# === discharge_via — the bridge to @glue/math_silicon ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING BRIDGE ACTION. Given an spd_matrix predicate + a
# morphism kind reference into @glue/math_silicon's admissible
# morphism family, returns the imperfect-wrapped result of composing
# the theorem's discharge with the LAPACK realization the morphism
# names.
#
# The morphism parameter is expected to name a member of
# `@arxiv/math/cholesky_decomposition.lapack_realization` (per
# `reality-algebra-math-and-glue.md` §2.1); typical values include:
#
#   @arxiv/math/cholesky_decomposition.lapack_realization.dpotrf
#   @arxiv/math/cholesky_decomposition.lapack_realization.zpotrf
#   @arxiv/math/cholesky_decomposition.lapack_realization.dpotrs
#
# @fate selects one within the @glue/math_silicon correspondence's
# restriction (precision: f64/f32/complex; conditioning: well-
# conditioned; layout: column-major); @glue.translate applies the
# selection via FFI to the SPD input; the result is the
# lower_triangular_matrix decomposition wrapped in imperfect —
# residual opacity (precision loss, conditioning warnings, LAPACK
# info-parameter reports) surfaced via transparency<precision>.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary; the realisation MUST
# invoke @glue.translate on the @glue/math_silicon correspondence,
# NOT a direct deterministic compute. The bilateral commitment
# (per recognition #37, Pask-agreement): the discharge respects
# the @glue morphism-category discipline.
discharge_via(a: spd_matrix, morphism: ref) -> imperfect<lower_triangular_matrix, ref, transparency(ref)>
requires cholesky_predicate_well_typed(a)
requires morphism_targets_math_silicon(morphism)
{ \ }

# === The bilateral requires obligations ===
#
# Downstream consumers declare bilateral agreement (per recognition
# #37, Pask-agreement) with the Cholesky predicate discipline by
# writing the obligations below.

# The spd_matrix carrier is well-typed as a Cholesky-admissible
# predicate iff: (a) field is a real or complex ordered field
# supporting the positive-definiteness quantifier; (b) dimension is
# strictly positive (Cholesky is not defined on the empty matrix);
# (c) the symmetric verdict is settled (`positive`); (d) the
# positive_def verdict is settled (`positive`). Inheritor consumers
# discharge by verifying each conjunct.
cholesky_predicate_well_typed(a: spd_matrix) -> verdict { \ }

# The morphism ref MUST name a member of @glue/math_silicon's
# admissible morphism family — i.e., a value under
# `@arxiv/math/cholesky_decomposition.lapack_realization`. This
# obligation closes the substrate-decl loop that @glue/math_silicon's
# correspondence opened: the theorem shard commits to naming
# morphisms only in the correspondence's typed restriction; @glue
# checks the restriction; @fate cannot select outside it. Inheritor
# consumers discharge by verifying the morphism ref's namespace.
morphism_targets_math_silicon(morphism: ref) -> verdict { \ }

# === The cholesky_witnessing inheritance predicate ===
#
# `cholesky_witnessing(d)` is the predicate consumer shards name in
# a `requires` clause to declare bilateral commitment that the
# cholesky_decomposition witness `d` is a valid Cholesky instance —
# i.e., d.input is Cholesky-admissible (cholesky_predicate_well_typed)
# AND d.output is a lower_triangular_matrix with strictly positive
# diagonal (encoded in the type) AND d.equality is a settled positive
# verdict (A = L · L^T holds within the numerical tolerance).
#
# Sibling to:
#   - `sheaf_laplacian.eigenvalue`'s spectral verdicts at the same
#     altitude (@epistemologic/math predicate family).
#   - `curvature.balanced_forman`'s per-edge verdicts.
#   - `glue.glue_witnessing(c)` (the morphism-correspondence
#     inheritance predicate the discharge_via bridge honors
#     transitively via @glue/math_silicon).
#
# No `requires cholesky_witnessing(...)` clauses at THIS shard. The
# predicate is declared; consumers add the clauses when they pull
# specific Cholesky instances (Reed's dpotrf empirical-crystal
# discharge; the first consumer to add the clause).
cholesky_witnessing(d: cholesky_decomposition) -> verdict { \ }

out @epistemologic/math/cholesky
out spd_matrix
out lower_triangular_matrix
out cholesky_decomposition
out verify
out discharge_via
out cholesky_predicate_well_typed
out morphism_targets_math_silicon
out cholesky_witnessing
